{:edb/db-before-t 2701 :edb/db-after-t 2702 :edb/tx-data [[13194139536014 50 #inst "2026-10-09T12:57:54.873Z" 13194139536014 true] [17592186105367 1145 "A random variable is a variable whose value is determined by the outcome of a trial or experiment. A *continuous* random variable is a random variable whose possible values are represented by an interval of real numbers.\n\nCommon examples of continuous random variables are shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040017/assets/05e3d893f6c069dce5b51dc7d9fa5830b44bca58fa071009548fde20ebe92c19.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I does *not* describe a continuous random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, but its possible values are the integers $0,1,2,3,\\ldots,$ which do not correspond to an interval of real numbers.\n\n- Statement II describes a continuous random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, and its possible values correspond to the real numbers in the interval $(0, \\infty).$\n\n- Statement III describes a continuous random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, and its possible values correspond to the real numbers in the interval $(0, 1\\,000].$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139536014 false] [17592186105367 1145 "A random variable is a variable whose value is determined by the outcome of a trial or experiment. A *continuous* random variable is a random variable whose possible values are represented by an interval of real numbers.\n\nCommon examples of continuous random variables are shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073533/assets/05e3d893f6c069dce5b51dc7d9fa5830b44bca58fa071009548fde20ebe92c19.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I does *not* describe a continuous random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, but its possible values are the integers $0,1,2,3,\\ldots,$ which do not correspond to an interval of real numbers.\n\n- Statement II describes a continuous random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, and its possible values correspond to the real numbers in the interval $(0, \\infty).$\n\n- Statement III describes a continuous random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, and its possible values correspond to the real numbers in the interval $(0, 1\\,000].$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139536014 true] [17592186105368 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040017/assets/26d09b87ebbabe4c1e1dcd8d5442b1d3efce216d7f404808a3777b8ffa3304ff.png)\n\nConsider the function $f(x)$ whose graph is shown above.\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{8}({x}^{2}+1),\\, & -1<x\\le 1, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x) \\geq 0$ for all $x$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\int_{-1}^1 f(x) \\,\\textrm{d}x = 1$\n\n- $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function" 13194139536014 false] [17592186105368 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073533/assets/26d09b87ebbabe4c1e1dcd8d5442b1d3efce216d7f404808a3777b8ffa3304ff.png)\n\nConsider the function $f(x)$ whose graph is shown above.\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073533/assets/7e6f6662d9597683762bcc48164e5d1227ae6a1a6bdea4243a3a3f4ca314e10a.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x) \\geq 0$ for all $x$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\int_{-1}^1 f(x) \\,\\textrm{d}x = 1$\n\n- $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function" 13194139536014 true] [17592186105377 1100 "Solve for $k$ given that the following function is a probability density function:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{3}+k{x}^{2},\\, & 0<x<1, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536014 false] [17592186105377 1100 "Solve for $k$ given that the following function is a probability density function:\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073533/assets/2bad707ee54e56c4171b99b13a36135522ab83069ab3b0291f3254850502226d.png)" 13194139536014 true] [17592186105781 1020 #edb/ref 17592186379413 13194139536014 true] [17592186105781 1020 #edb/ref 17592186379420 13194139536014 true] [17592186105781 1020 #edb/ref 17592186379455 13194139536014 true] [17592186105782 1020 #edb/ref 17592186379427 13194139536014 true] [17592186105782 1020 #edb/ref 17592186379434 13194139536014 true] [17592186105807 1020 #edb/ref 17592186379441 13194139536014 true] [17592186105807 1020 #edb/ref 17592186379448 13194139536014 true] [17592186105979 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139536014 true] [17592186105979 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073533/assets/8835c085ca34d59f5c79786e12ed67cb33ccfdcfefd706dcc83ca2be0239670f.png)\n\nConsider the function $f(x)$ whose graph is shown above.\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2},\\, & 0<x<1, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x$\n- ${\\int }_{0}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n- $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function" 13194139536014 true] [17592186105979 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1347/Source/Images/q-158315.png)\n\nConsider the function $f(x)$ whose graph is shown above.\n$f(x) = {3x^{2},\\begin{vmatrix}0 < x < 1, \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $f(x) \\ge 0$ for all $x$\n2. $∫_{0}^{1}f(x)dx = 1$\n3. $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function" 13194139536014 false] [17592186105979 1145 "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n\n- ${\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. From the graph, we see that $f(x)$ is nonnegative for all $x.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[{x}^{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =[{1}^{3}]-[{0}^{3}] \\\\  & =1-0 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statements I and II are true, $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" 13194139536014 true] [17592186105980 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139536014 true] [17592186105980 1100 "Solve for $k$ given that the following function is a probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+k,\\, & 0<x<2, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536014 true] [17592186105980 1100 "Solve for $k$ given that the following function is a probability density function:\n\n$f(x) = {\\frac{x^{2}}{4} + k,\\begin{vmatrix}0 < x < 2, \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise$." 13194139536014 false] [17592186105980 1145 "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n\n- ${\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n\nFor the given function, the second condition states that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{2}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+k)\\,\\text{d}x=1.\n$$\n\nComputing the integral on the left-hand side, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+k)\\,\\text{d}x & ={[\\frac{1}{12}{x}^{3}+kx]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =[\\frac{8}{12}+2k]-[0+0] \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}+2k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{3}+2k=1\\,⟹\\,k=\\frac{1}{6}.\n$$" 13194139536014 true] [17592186106458 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139536014 true] [17592186